675 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2548 0 R
/E (Identify risks: Risks are defined as materials or suppliers whose disruption could cause very high or even existential damage. Specific threats or probabilities are not the starting point. Using Bill of Materials analysis, the affected products and revenue streams are identified, and potential damage is quantified \(e.g., loss of sales or margin\), including additional impacts such as penalties, recovery costs, or consequential losses.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [41]
>>
endobj
676 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2549 0 R
/E (Analyze and evaluate risks: High-impact materials and suppliers are assessed in terms of substitutability. If short-term alternatives exist, the risk is limited. Otherwise, the resilience gap is determined, defined as the time difference between available inventory and the availability of a replacement source. This gap represents the actual risk exposure.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [42]
>>
endobj
677 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2550 0 R
/E (Manage and monitor risks: Preventive measures are defined and implemented where required, similar to the cause-oriented approach. These measures can be anchored in the Procurement Framework Strategy or handled as operational improvements. Ongoing monitoring is essential to track risk developments and identify new exposures, with a full impact-oriented review conducted at least annually.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [43]
>>
endobj
